and this notice does not describe that configuration.

1. Crypto++ 8.9.0

  What it is     the cryptographic implementation used by this module.
                 Public headers do not expose CryptoPP::
  How it is used compiled into libStormByte-Crypto
  Source         https://github.com/weidai11/cryptopp
                 commit 843d74c7c97f9e19a615b8ff3c0ca06599ca501b
                 ("Prepare for Crypto++ 8.9 release")
  License        COPYING.CryptoPP, which is License.txt of that commit,
                 copied verbatim

  License.txt places individual files in the public domain and licenses the
  compilation under the Boost Software License 1.0. The Boost Software
  License's own condition about keeping the license text applies to copies
  that are not solely compiler-generated object code. The text is included
  here anyway.

  poly1305.cpp is compiled into this library on every platform. Its header
  carries the CRYPTOGAMS copyright (2006-2017, appro@openssl.org) and the
  redistribution conditions that License.txt also reproduces. Those
  conditions require the notice to travel with a binary distribution.
  COPYING.CryptoPP is that notice, as upstream wrote it, including where
  the upstream file stops.

  The 32-bit ARM CRYPTOGAMS assembly named in License.txt (aes_armv4.S,
  sha1_armv4.S, sha256_armv4.S) is not built for the published archives
  (Windows x64, Linux x64, macOS arm64). The zlib, gzip and deflate sources
  in that tree are Wei Dai's public-domain reimplementation. They are not
  the zlib library and they are not libbzip2.

  On Windows the build applies
  thirdparty/Crypto++/0001-msvc-drop-stdext.patch. The patch avoids
  stdext in integer.cpp and zdeflate.cpp. It adds no source file and it
  does not change License.txt.

